    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Enlightenment was a movement of writers, scientists and reformers who held that reason, evidence and free enquiry, not tradition or authority alone, should guide knowledge, belief and government.</li><li>Its thinkers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, argued for toleration, natural rights, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>, freer trade and the reform of cruel punishments.</li><li>Its ideas guided reforming monarchs and inspired the declarations of rights of 1776 and 1789, though the rights it proclaimed long excluded most women and the enslaved.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> finished her French translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>, with a long commentary of her own, in 1749, the year she died; published complete in 1759, it is still the standard French version.</p></aside>
    </section>

