    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1687</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> publishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>, setting out the laws of motion and universal gravitation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1689</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> publishes his Two Treatises of Government and An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1748</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> publishes The Spirit of the Laws, with its case for separating the powers of government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1751</span><span class="rf-text">The first volume of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a>, edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> and Jean le Rond d’Alembert, appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> publishes The Social Contract and Emile; both are banned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> publishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a>; the American Declaration of Independence proclaims equal rights</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1784</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> answers the question “What is Enlightenment?” with the motto “Sapere aude”, dare to know</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">The French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> adopts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="John Locke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_Kneller_-_Portrait_of_John_Locke_(Hermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godfrey Kneller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the later 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was emerging from more than a century of religious war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> had ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, but states still enforced a single faith and persecuted dissenters: in 1685 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> revoked the Edict of Nantes and drove many thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>, French Protestants, into exile. Many settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, whose relatively free presses printed books that could not appear elsewhere. There the Huguenot refugee Pierre Bayle published his Historical and Critical Dictionary in 1697, whose sceptical notes on the errors of past authorities became a storehouse for later critics of dogma, and there <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baruch-spinoza">Baruch Spinoza</a> had argued in his Theological-Political Treatise of 1670 that the Bible should be read as a historical document and that the state should allow freedom of thought.</p><p>Natural science supplied a new model of knowledge. The work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a> had overturned the ancient picture of the cosmos, and learned societies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society of London</a>, founded in 1660, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Academy of Sciences, founded in 1666, organised experiment and the exchange of results. In 1687 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica</a> showed that the same few mathematical laws governed falling bodies on earth and the motions of the planets. To many readers Newton proved that patient observation and reason could uncover the order of nature, and they hoped to find similar laws of human society.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> offered a political example. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688–1689 brought a Bill of Rights and an Act of Toleration for Protestant dissenters, and the lapse of the Licensing Act in 1695 ended censorship before publication. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, back from exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Republic, published in 1689 (though their title pages bear the date 1690) An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, which argued that the mind begins as “white paper” furnished by experience, and Two Treatises of Government, which grounded government in the consent of the governed. Across Europe a growing reading public met in coffee houses, clubs and academies and read newspapers, periodicals and travellers’ accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> that showed other ways of living. The French historian Paul Hazard later called the years from 1680 to 1715 “the crisis of the European mind”, the moment when old certainties began to give way.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant.jpg" alt="Immanuel Kant" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Gottlieb Becker (1720-1782), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Enlightenment had no single programme, party or creed, and its thinkers often quarrelled. What united them was confidence that human reason, applied to experience, could improve both knowledge and the conditions of life. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> defined enlightenment as “man’s emergence from his self-incurred immaturity”, the inability to use one’s own understanding without another’s guidance, and gave it a motto from the Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, “Sapere aude”, dare to know, which he rendered as “Have courage to use your own understanding!” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> wrote that the purpose of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> was “to change the way people think”.</p><p>In religion the reformers attacked what they called superstition, fanaticism and persecution. Locke’s Letter Concerning Toleration of 1689 argued that the state had no business forcing belief, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>’s Treatise on Tolerance of 1763 was written after the Protestant merchant Jean Calas was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 1762 on a false charge of murdering his son to stop him becoming a Catholic. Voltaire ended many letters with the cry “Écrasez l’infâme”, “crush the infamous thing”, aimed at intolerance and the power of the Church. Few of the leading thinkers were atheists, though some were, notably Baron d’Holbach, whose System of Nature appeared in 1770. Many were deists who believed in a creator who did not intervene in the world, and others, such as the Jewish philosopher Moses Mendelssohn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and many Protestant and Catholic clergy, sought to reconcile reason with revealed faith.</p><p>In politics the Enlightenment produced no single model but a set of lasting ideas. Locke grounded government in natural rights to life, liberty and property and in the consent of the governed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> urged the separation of legislative, executive and judicial power; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, whose The Social Contract opens “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”, placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the people and their general will. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">On Crimes and Punishments</a> of 1764 condemned torture and the death penalty. The French physiocrats called for free trade in grain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a> of 1776, argued that the division of labour and free exchange create wealth. Many writers called for useful knowledge, wider education and the freedom of the press, and some, like the Marquis de Condorcet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a>, extended the argument for equal rights to women.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/encyclopedie.jpg" alt="Encyclopédie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Encyclopedie_de_D%27Alembert_et_Diderot_-_Premiere_Page_-_ENC_1-NA5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Long List of Contributors to the Encyclopédie, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement’s first centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, but French writers soon took up their ideas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>’s Persian Letters of 1721 satirised French society through the eyes of two Persian travellers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, exiled to England from 1726 to 1728, returned an admirer of English toleration, of Locke and of Newton, and his Philosophical Letters of 1734 were condemned and burned by order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>. At Cirey, the château of the du Châtelet family, he and his companion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> worked to spread Newton’s physics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The high Enlightenment opened around the middle of the century. In 1748 Montesquieu published The Spirit of the Laws, and in 1751 the first volume of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> appeared, edited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> and the mathematician Jean le Rond d’Alembert. It was briefly suspended in 1752, and in 1759 the government revoked its licence to publish, but Diderot, left as sole editor when d’Alembert withdrew, carried on in secret: the last ten volumes of text came out together in 1765 and the plates by 1772, twenty-eight volumes with more than 70,000 articles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a>, which struck on 1 November, shook optimistic faith in a benevolent order of the world; Voltaire answered it with a poem in 1756 and with his satire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> in 1759. In 1762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>’s The Social Contract and Emile were banned in France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and Rousseau fled to escape arrest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a> flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment">Italian Enlightenment</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> Kant published his Critique of Pure Reason in 1781.</p><p>Rulers took up the new ideas for their own ends. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of Prussia corresponded with Voltaire and kept him at his court from 1750 to 1753; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> based her Instruction of 1767 on Montesquieu and Beccaria; in Portugal the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> after the earthquake on a regular plan and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1759. In America, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence</a> of 1776, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> took a leading part in framing the constitution of 1787. In France the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> in August 1789, weeks after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille">Storming of the Bastille</a>, and in Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> reformers passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a> of 1793–1794, the Napoleonic Wars and the restoration of monarchies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 are usually taken to close the age.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Largillière, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The best-known figures were the French philosophes, men of letters who wrote for a broad public rather than for universities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, born François-Marie Arouet, wrote plays, histories, satires and pamphlets, campaigned for the victims of injustice and spent his last two decades at Ferney near the Swiss border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> was a nobleman and magistrate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a>, the son of a cutler from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>, was imprisoned at Vincennes in 1749 for his writings and gave a quarter of a century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, a watchmaker’s son from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, won fame in 1750 with a prize essay for the Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, then quarrelled with most of the other philosophes and became the movement’s most searching critic of luxury and inequality.</p><p>Other centres had their own leaders. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a>, sceptic and historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and author of The Theory of Moral Sentiments and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a>, turned moral philosophy towards the study of society. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> pleaded for tolerance among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and Muslims in his play Nathan the Wise of 1779, and Kant, who taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a>, examined the limits of reason itself. Beccaria wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, printer, scientist of electricity and diplomat, became the American Enlightenment’s most famous figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Women were prominent though rarely acknowledged as equals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> translated Newton’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> into French with her own commentary. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, hostesses such as Madame Geoffrin and Julie de Lespinasse held salons where writers, nobles and foreign visitors met. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges">Olympe de Gouges</a> answered the French declaration of 1789 with a Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen in 1791, and in 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a> published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-vindication-of-the-rights-of-woman">A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</a>. Beyond these names the movement lived among thousands of readers: the members of provincial academies, Masonic lodges and reading societies, the customers of coffee houses, and the printers and booksellers who sold its books, including the many forbidden works smuggled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the Netherlands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen.jpg" alt="Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_the_Rights_of_Man_and_of_the_Citizen_in_1789.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Parts of the Enlightenment’s programme became law within its own age. Toleration advanced with Joseph II’s Patent of Toleration of 1781 for Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Statute for Religious Freedom of 1786 and the edict of 1787 that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">French Protestants</a> civil status. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> abolished judicial torture, and in 1786 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Pietro Leopoldo</a>, later the Emperor Leopold II, abolished the death penalty. The constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of 1787 divided power as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> had urged, and the declarations of 1776 and 1789 proclaimed natural rights. Academies, encyclopaedias, museums and scientific societies multiplied, and the metric system, adopted in revolutionary France in the 1790s, was a product of the same wish for rational order.</p><p>The limits were as plain. Enlightened monarchs strengthened their states more than their subjects’ liberties: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>’s commission to draft a new law code produced none, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> spread in her empire; Joseph II’s reforms provoked revolt in the Austrian Netherlands and resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and many were withdrawn, some by Joseph himself shortly before his death in 1790. Many philosophes condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> a violation of natural law, yet the Atlantic trade reached its height in the 18th century. Hume, Kant and other writers set out theories that ranked human races, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>’s Emile prescribed for girls an education designed to serve men. In practice the new political rights went first, and for long only, to men, and usually to men of property.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> divided the movement’s heirs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>’s Reflections on the Revolution in France of 1790 blamed abstract theorising for the destruction of a traditional order, and the Terror seemed to many to confirm his warning: Condorcet, who wrote his Sketch for a Historical Picture of the Progress of the Human Mind while in hiding, died in prison in 1794 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges">Olympe de Gouges</a> was guillotined in 1793. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, ruling by force, carried the civil equality and secular law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a> of 1804 across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Romantic writers turned against what they saw as the cold rule of reason, but the Enlightenment’s ideas lived on in liberalism, in the utilitarianism of Jeremy Bentham and in the sciences of society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights.jpg" alt="Universal Declaration of Human Rights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleanor_Roosevelt_UDHR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDR Presidential Library &amp; Museum, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Enlightenment shaped much of the modern world’s vocabulary of politics: natural and human rights, the separation of powers, government by consent, freedom of conscience and of the press, and the secular state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a> of 1948 stands in the line of the declarations of 1776 and 1789. Its confidence in research and useful knowledge shaped universities, scientific institutions and modern disciplines such as economics and sociology, which grew from its “science of man”, and its encyclopaedias, among them the first Encyclopaedia Britannica, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> from 1768, were the ancestors of modern works of reference.</p><p>Historians have judged it in sharply different ways. Ernst Cassirer’s The Philosophy of the Enlightenment of 1932 presented it as a confident and unified project of reason. Writing in exile during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Max Horkheimer and Theodor Adorno argued in Dialectic of Enlightenment that reason reduced to an instrument of control could turn into a new kind of domination. Peter Gay’s The Enlightenment: An Interpretation of 1966–1969 portrayed the philosophes as a loose family of critics who joined the classics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to modern science in the cause of freedom. Later historians such as Franco Venturi, J. G. A. Pocock and Roy Porter stressed its national and religious variety, Pocock preferring to speak of Enlightenments in the plural, while Robert Darnton studied the book trade and the hack writers below the famous names.</p><p>Debate continues. Jonathan Israel has argued that a “radical Enlightenment” rooted in Spinoza, rather than the moderate mainstream of Locke and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, did most to promote democracy, equality and freedom of thought, a thesis many historians dispute. Others have studied the Catholic and Jewish Enlightenments, the movement’s reach into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the wider world, and its entanglement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and empire. Defenders and critics alike still treat it as the reference point for arguments about reason, tolerance and universal rights.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Enlightenment was a movement of ideas in 17th- and 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> that put reason, evidence and free enquiry above tradition and authority. Its thinkers argued for toleration, natural rights, the separation of powers and humane laws, and their ideas shaped the American and French revolutions and modern liberal government, though the rights they proclaimed long excluded women and the enslaved.</em></p>

